            <ArticleTitle>Helicobacter pylori: Eradication or Preservation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3410/M4-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Helicobacter pylori infects about 50% of the world's population and inevitably results in the development of gastritis. Of those infected, about 10% develop peptic ulcer disease and roughly 1% develop gastric cancer. Conversely, some take the view that H. pylori infection provides some protection against gastro-esophageal reflux disease and possibly asthma. This review aims to explore the case for and against eradication of the bacterium using a &quot;test and treat&quot; approach amongst the general population.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
